  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1115 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nLate evening water vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air \nanalysis show modest southwesterly flow is in place atop the central \nplains between a ridge over Dixie Alley and a trough making landfall \nalong the Pacific Northwest. At the surface, light northerly winds \nin the wake of a strong cold front are spread out across southwest \nKS, which brought brief relief from the heat earlier this afternoon \nand will yield Wednesday morning lows in the upper 50s to mid 60s. \nUnfortunately, the cool air will not be here to stay as a lee \ncyclone in eastern CO/WY daytime tomorrow will renew southerly flow \nthrough the southern and central plains and combine with gradually \nclearing skies to support afternoon temperatures warming back into \nthe upper 80s/low 90s for most locations. A few small areas of mid-\n80s are possible near I-70 where cloud cover will persist the \nlongest. Thunderstorm potential tomorrow afternoon/evening will also \nbe muted/non-existent as mid-level height rises suppress convective \nactivity.\n\nDaytime Thursday, short range ensembles agree the strong upper level \nridge will encompass all areas from the southern plains \nnortheastward into the central Midwest and eastward through the east \ncoast, with the aforementioned trough loitering over the Pacific \nNorthwest. The resulting southwesterly flow across the central \nRockies will again support surface lee troughing in northeast CO, \nallowing the southerly winds over southwest KS to acquire a westerly \ncomponent. These downsloping winds along with strong solar \ninsolation will engender afternoon highs returning to the mid/upper \n90s, with some low 100s possible within the Red Hills.\n\nFriday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles suggest \nthe upper level trough will finally obtain some easterly momentum \nand slowly meander across the northern CONUS through mid-next week, \nbreaking down the strong upper ridge in the process. As this \nsynoptic evolution unfolds, southwest KS will at long last enjoy the \nfirst prolonged cooldown of the fall season as a strong cold front \nmoves through our area sometime late Saturday or early Sunday. NBM \nhighs on Friday/Saturday cool from the mid/upper 90s into the mid \n70s to mid 80s on Sunday, and stay in that range through Wednesday. \nConfidence is also high in precipitation accompanying the cold \nfropa as NBM pops in the likely (55-74%) and definite (75-100%) \ncategories exist for nearly all zones early Sunday.",
